   "why": "along a scan that compresses or stretches one coordinate monotonically, the energy should not FALL: a drop means a neighbouring point was trapped in a worse arrangement, which a backward pass fixes. A steep but smooth rise is physics, not an artefact, so plain step size is the wrong test"
  },
  {
   "check": "chalcogen_barrier",
   "value": 0.6351619511437008,
   "label": "se_s xTB",
   "low": 2.0,
   "high": 30.0,
   "unit": "kcal/mol",
   "why": "thiolate-disulfide exchange in water runs on a timescale of milliseconds to seconds at millimolar thiol, so a barrier much above 30 contradicts the measured second-order rates, and one below 2 would mean no barrier at all, which contradicts their being second order in the first place"
  },
  {
   "check": "exchange_well",
   "value": 9.298223663527272,
   "label": "se_s symmetric species",
   "low": 0.0,
   "high": 2.0,
   "unit": "kcal/mol",
   "why": "how far the symmetric arrangement of a displacement sits BELOW the reactant complex. For an S_N2 it should be about zero, because that arrangement IS the transition state. GFN2-xTB puts it 8 kcal/mol lower for thiolate plus disulfide, making the hypervalent trisulfur anion a bound species and the whole surface a well instead of a barrier. That is the same over-stabilisation of a thiolate the second manuscript found, and here it changes the topology rather than just the number, so no saddle search seeded from this surface can succeed"
  }
